{
  "name": "How Long Until My New Drywall is Ready for Paint?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting new drywall installed is exciting, but then comes the waiting game before you can slap on that fresh coat of paint. It's not just about hanging the sheets; there's a crucial drying and finishing period that you really can't rush. Typically, from the start of hanging the drywall until it's actually paint-ready, you're looking at about 15 days. This isn't just arbitrary; it's about making sure all those layers of joint compound have fully dried and cured. You've got the initial mudding over the tape, then subsequent coats, and sanding in between each. Each layer needs its own time to dry completely, and this is highly dependent on the temperature and humidity in your home. If the environment is too humid or cold, it'll take even longer. Rushing this process can lead to problems like cracking, bubbling, or an uneven finish down the line, which nobody wants! So, while it's tempting to speed things up, patience here truly pays off with a smooth, durable surface for your paint. A professional installer will always factor in these drying times to ensure a high-quality finish.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
